    "commentary": {
      "title": "Commentary: what the chart shows",
      "points": [
        {
          "head": "Steady growth, then a step up after 2021",
          "text": "Commitments rose from about €141bn in 2010 to a peak of €199bn in 2025. Payments follow commitments with a delay, because most EU programmes pay out over several years."
        },
        {
          "head": "Why payments dropped in 2024",
          "text": "The old 2014–2020 regional and social fund programmes had mostly finished paying out, while the new 2021–2027 cohesion programmes only started after all of them were approved at the end of 2022, so few bills came in yet. Cohesion payments fell from €58.1bn (2023) to €33.7bn (2024). At the same time, national administrations focused on the COVID-19 recovery fund (RRF, €59.9bn spent in 2024, paid outside the yearly budget), which may have delayed cohesion programmes. Result: 2024 payments were set at €142.6bn, 13.7% below 2023."
        },
        {
          "head": "Why payments jump in 2025–2026",
          "text": "The 2021–2027 programmes are now at cruising speed and the recovery fund ends in 2026, so payments climb to €190.1bn in 2026, almost equal to commitments."
        },
        {
          "head": "What it means for our clients",
          "text": "2025–2027 is the busiest period for national and regional calls funded by the 2021–2027 cohesion programmes: a good window to prepare projects."
        }
      ],
